s  quickly and easily so to help them out  we built our first prototype we built it  using PI spark and Zeppelin two great  open source technologies out of Apache  it looks something like this  I would like to take a minute to walk  through it so this was HVAC runtime is  calculated with Pi spark for those of  you who aren't familiar HVAC stands for  heating ventilation and air conditioning  so it's a measure of how much time your  furnace or how much time your air  conditioner was running throughout the  course of a day you can see on the  x-axis here we've got a fraction of day  with HVAC running this is a histogram on  the y-axis you've got the fraction of  the device population so all the way on  the right you see that about 5% of the  population was running a hundred percent  of the day all the way on the left you  see about 20 percent of the population  was running not at all during the course  of the day this might be alarming  except for that this data came from a  set of test devices so that leads into  one of the major complexities that we  found with this system it was really  costly to run analyses like this on all  of production we had the capacity to run  it on production and for the most  important works those we did but if you  wanted to think about other types of  work those it became really tricky to do  so one of the main reasons this was so  tricky is that our storage was coupled  to our compute so a less we picked  exactly the right instance type with  exactly the right amount of RAM exactly  the right amount of storage and exactly  the right number of CPUs we always had  too much of one and not enough of the  other I was describing this problem to  my roommate as I was preparing this talk  and she mentioned that she had the same  problem except for she had it when she  went to the store and she bought chips  and she bought salsa so no matter what  she always said too much one and not  enough for the other we've got the same  problem at nest  except for we've got it with storage and  compute of course that leads to frequent  resource contention we had one cluster  where the data were located we are in  both high priority workflows on it but  also lower priority analytics tasks  which meant that the lower priority  analytics tasks were frequently  preempted or bumped from the work though  this meant that our analytics team had  to either go back and reschedule or  rerun those workflows or have gaps in  their analysis that also meant that our  team took on the role of a part-time  sysadmin we got excuse me we got many  phone calls saying hey this works those  just been  it turn out the memory can you help us  get it running again which wasn't a  great working environment for the team  there are a number of operational  complexities that we ran into as well so  PI spark and Zeppelin were great tools  but they weren't built to do this sort  of dashboarding so we ended up building  a separate service that was responsible  for generating JSON blobs that  represented these templates that we  wanted to create loading them into the  notebook cache of the Zeppelin monitor  restarting the service so the Zeppelin  monitor picked up on the new notebooks  this worked well for one users but you  can imagine his multiple users started  trying to use the system and it was  frequently restarting all the time it  wasn't a great experience for them  additionally there was a constraint that  there was a maximum number of notebooks  that could be loaded on the machine at  once so we had to vote a second service  that was responsible for backing these  notebooks up to durable store and then  pulling them back in when we needed them  it wasn't a great experience for the  people who wanted to see the data this  next point is an especially important  one I want to take a moment to talk  about it  one of the things that we care most  about nest is protecting our users data  and protecting our users privacy and  many of the mechanisms that from the  systems that we were using provided only  clunky authentication and authorization  which made it difficult to do the fast  and flexible data analysis to return  value to our customers similar problem  was that it became very difficult to  build analyses back to different teams  so for example imagine that the  thermostat team came to us and said hey  we've got this great new data type that  we want to store we know that your  cluster is full we know that we're gonna  have to expand the cluster what's it  gonna take to load that data type in it  was difficult to figure out exactly how  much that extra space would cost  we had some heuristics to help us come  up with estimates they always proved to  either be too high or too low and so the  end we ended up building most of the  cost back to our team which was great  from a billing perspective or a  management perspective but terrible from  a team bottom line perspective and  finally if you were an analytics team  that had already generated some data  that you wanted to use to augment or  color your analysis it was difficult to  get data into the system you had to  learn a new set of tools you had to get  the appropriate  permissions and then you had to figure  out how to join and link those two  together so I'd like to take a little  bit of time to talk about the solution  that we came up with so on the left here  this is a zoomed in view of the  architecture that we discussed  previously and it starts with the events  in cloud bigquery up at the top so those  events are the same events that were  loaded from Kafka into bigquery by  dataflow once we've got the data  available in bigquery we then built a  processing library on top of it the  processing library was responsible for  two different pieces one there's a  number of nest specific nuances in our  data set so the processing law library  was aware of those nuances and provided  a higher level abstraction on top of  them to make it easier to work with and  to one of the most frequent kinds of  analyses that we do at nest is time  series analysis so it provided a number  of very useful higher level abstractions  for time series analysis one of the  really nice things as Sam mentioned was  that even though we're running in cloud  data file the code is just Apache beam  is completely open source completely  portable so if we wanted to we could  pull this cut code out of data flow and  run it elsewhere run it on our own flesh  to run it wherever we might want to  we're not planning on doing that we love  working with data flow but it is really  nice to have the flexibility to do that  wherever we do the computation after  it's complete we load the data back into  bigquery for further processing or  analysis and from there there's a number  of really nice integrations that make it  easy to visualize two of my favorites  are up on the screen you've got data  studio and data lab but it's also got  really easy connectors for Excel or  tableau or Google sheets or whatever you  might want to use to take a look at it  so I'd like to talk about two advantage  or a couple of advantages from using the  system first and foremost was also auto  scaling this auto scaling was huge for  us it made it really easy for us to  develop code locally to test code  locally and then to push it out to our  production cluster to run at scale by  only changing a few configs and to  demonstrate how powerful this was I'd  like to tell a short story we had a  camera systems engineer who was a great  engineer but again didn't have too much  familiarity with building services or  analytics workflows he was able to get  up and running using the libraries we  created in only a day so there was a  huge operational  advantage from using this system part of  the reason it was such a big win was  that storage or we were finally able to  decouple storage from compute so as I  mentioned before all of this storage was  handled by bigquery all of the compute  was handled by dataflow so we could add  new logs to the system without worrying  about scaling out data flow or we could  add new analysis pipelines without  worried about Skerritt scaling out  bigquery and finally because everything  runs as a managed service there was no  more resource contention with production  jobs which meant that the analytics  teams could run their jobs without  worrying about backfill but excuse me  without worrying about backfill and also  without worrying about having winners or  missing windows in their data sets and  that our team got many fewer late-night  phone calls there are a number of  operational advantages that well this  first one is a big one they've got this  really nicely defined access control  model that made it really easy for us to  grant people access to the right data at  the right time and only when they need  it so their access control model was  great and increased our operating  security a similar system we're finally  able to build analyses by team so  whenever you kick off a work flow you're  able to set a billing can shape or a  bidding ID that means that the teams  that kickoff works those are built for  only the computer cycles they use and  nothing more and finally as we mentioned  previously really great options for data  visualization data studio data lab to  blow anything that you want to work with  is available so I'd like to revisit one  of the example use cases that we talked  about before this is the same age factor  runtime analysis except for this time  it's computed with a cloud data file so  on the x-axis you see the day that it  ran on the y-axis you see the fraction  of time that your furnace or air  conditioner was on it starts out low in  October while things are still pretty  warm it increases through January 10th  where there was a huge storm in the  Northeast and then it falls back off as  things start to warm up in the spring  this is a really simple computation but  this graph hides a really important fact  this is one of the first times that we  were able to run an analysis like this  on all of production so all of those  sampling pieces that I talked before  where you're worried about getting the  wrong population or missing population  or masking populations we don't have to  worry about  this is great if you want to build more  complicated or more nuanced machine  learning models on top of the data you  can be sure that you're hitting all of  the points so with that I want to  highlight that even a simple computation  like the one that I just mentioned even  just figuring out HVAC on and off  periods can have some really positive  returns and value for our customers this  is one of my favorite products that we  offer at nest this is the monthly Energy  Report so every month we email our nests  teachers and say how much energy used  how much energy used as compared to last  month we try to sprinkle a little  machine-learning on top of it to say why  your energy use has changed maybe it got  colder maybe the weather changed maybe  you adjusted your thermostat to be more  comfortable in your home we also provide  one of my favorite statistics that nest  so every month we give a summary of how  much energy people have saved by  installing nests across the entire  population this is the one from January  so this is current through January 31st  since October 11 I'm sorry October 2011  nesters have saved eleven point three  billion kilowatt hours which is enough  energy to freeze all of the ice  necessary for 2 million ice skating  rinks so that's a pretty cool insight  that comes out of relatively  straightforward data analysis once  you're able to perform it on the entire  fleet it's also a lot of energy to save  simply by installing a thermostat fast  inflexible data analysis also lets us  help prove how efficient our devices are  so we're really proud to announce that  nest was the first thermostat in America  with an Energy Star certification and  some of the fast and flexible data  analysis techniques that I talked about  helped us be able to prove this  efficiency out so with that I'd like to  wrap up using the technologies available  to us within GCP  provided an easy pass from development  to production paved by shared and  scalable tooling throughout so that  means that an algorithms engineer like  Kevin can develop and prototype data  sets using one set of tools and then  take those same tools to do analysis  when he deploys the algorithm to  production there's no need to learn  another tool set for doing data at scale  or for doing smaller data allows  locally the access control models to  provide give the right people the right  access to the right data at the right  time and similarly the billing  operations that they allow for allows  you to build the right teams for what  they use and more importantly for only  what they use but most importantly the  data availability and access control is  provided by GCP help us further our  mission of creating the home that cares  so with that I'd like to thank you to  all of the team members at nest and  Google who contributed to these projects  these weren't built by a single person  we have a great engineering engineering  team that nests of them who are here  today that are wonderful to work with  we've got a great engineering team at  Google including people like Sam who  have helped us along the process so  thank you to both and I'd also like to  thank you in the audience roots staying  in listening will be outside after the  talk feel free come up ask any questions  that we might have say about Mike I  mentioned we've also got some energy  other data services engineers from nest  who can help to field questions or  provide more information so thank you  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

